1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,800
It is a winter night in 1992,

2
00:00:02,260 --> 00:00:03,990
a little after three in the morning.

3
00:00:04,020 --> 00:00:05,240
A federal agent is parked

4
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:06,240
on a public street,

5
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:08,180
looking at an ordinary house across

6
00:00:08,180 --> 00:00:09,070
the road.

7
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:10,574
He never knocks on the door.

8
00:00:10,780 --> 00:00:12,306
He never steps onto the lawn.

9
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:13,900
He just raises a device

10
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:15,240
to his eyes, points

11
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:16,140
it at the home,

12
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:17,720
and reads the heat coming off

13
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:18,620
the walls.

14
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:20,740
From that heat, he decides he knows

15
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:23,990
what is happening inside.

16
00:00:23,991 --> 00:00:24,491
No warrant.

17
00:00:24,492 --> 00:00:26,920
No foot on the property.
Just a machine, a wall,

18
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:28,980
and a guess about a stranger's private

19
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:29,880
life.

20
00:00:30,380 --> 00:00:34,700
Nine years later, the Supreme Court had to answer
one question about that quiet moment:

21
00:00:34,940 --> 00:00:35,840
was it a search?

22
00:00:36,540 --> 00:00:40,220
We treat the home as the one place the government
cannot reach without a warrant.

23
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:43,450
Your walls are supposed to be the
edge of your private world.

24
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:45,440
But technology has a way of slipping

25
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:46,620
around old rules.

26
00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:48,860
A thermal camera does not break down

27
00:00:48,860 --> 00:00:50,460
your door or pick your lock.

28
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:53,020
It just measures the warmth your house

29
00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:55,060
gives off, and turns that warmth

30
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:56,640
into a picture of what you might

31
00:00:56,640 --> 00:00:58,940
be doing behind the walls.

32
00:00:58,941 --> 00:01:01,960
Over the next twelve minutes: the
freezing night that started it,

33
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:04,520
the hidden operation the heat gave away,

34
00:01:05,020 --> 00:01:06,900
and the line the Court finally drew

35
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:09,234
at the front door of every home in America.

36
00:01:09,320 --> 00:01:11,260
The house sat on Rhododendron Drive

37
00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:12,998
in Florence, Oregon —

38
00:01:12,999 --> 00:01:14,262
part of a small triplex,

39
00:01:14,860 --> 00:01:17,124
three homes joined under one roof.

40
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:19,160
A man named Danny Kyllo lived

41
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:20,060
in one of them.

42
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:25,720
Federal agents had come to suspect that he was
growing marijuana inside, under lights.

43
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:28,293
Growing marijuana indoors takes light —

44
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:29,440
a great deal of it.

45
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,320
Indoor growers run high-intensity lamps

46
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:33,440
for hours at a time,

47
00:01:33,860 --> 00:01:35,940
and those lamps throw off serious heat

48
00:01:35,941 --> 00:01:36,441
.

49
00:01:36,442 --> 00:01:37,758
And heat is hard to hide.

50
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:39,800
It seeps through walls and roofs

51
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:41,480
and leaks out into the cold night

52
00:01:41,480 --> 00:01:42,840
air, whether you want

53
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:44,056
it to or not.

54
00:01:44,240 --> 00:01:46,140
So on January 16,

55
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:47,920
1992, a little after three

56
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:49,980
in the morning, an agent named William

57
00:01:49,980 --> 00:01:51,500
Elliott sat in a car across

58
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:53,605
the street with a thermal imaging device

59
00:01:54,953 --> 00:01:55,253
—

60
00:01:55,254 --> 00:01:57,065
an Agema Thermovision 210.

61
00:01:57,066 --> 00:01:59,570
A thermal imager does not see through walls.

62
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:00,500
It sees heat.

63
00:02:01,140 --> 00:02:03,580
Almost every object gives
off infrared radiation

64
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:05,220
that human eyes cannot detect,

65
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:07,880
and the device turns that invisible warmth

66
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:09,385
into a picture —

67
00:02:09,386 --> 00:02:11,520
black for cool, white for hot,

68
00:02:12,020 --> 00:02:13,733
gray for everything in between.

69
00:02:13,940 --> 00:02:16,906
The scan took only a few minutes,
and the picture told a story.

70
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:18,980
The roof over the garage and one

71
00:02:18,980 --> 00:02:21,100
side wall of Kyllo's home glowed far

72
00:02:21,100 --> 00:02:22,830
hotter than the rest of the house,

73
00:02:22,860 --> 00:02:27,127
and hotter than the neighboring
units under the same roof.

74
00:02:27,128 --> 00:02:30,884
To Elliott, that pattern meant one thing:
powerful grow lamps, running inside.

75
00:02:31,060 --> 00:02:31,960
He never went in.

76
00:02:32,340 --> 00:02:33,708
He never crossed the yard.

77
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:36,800
He read the outside of the house,
in the dark, and drew a firm

78
00:02:36,800 --> 00:02:38,274
conclusion about the inside.

79
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:40,880
That conclusion did real work.

80
00:02:41,340 --> 00:02:43,000
Along with a tip from an informant

81
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:44,880
and unusually high electric bills,

82
00:02:45,340 --> 00:02:47,480
the thermal scan helped Elliott convince

83
00:02:47,480 --> 00:02:49,164
a judge to sign a search warrant

84
00:02:49,368 --> 00:02:49,668
.

85
00:02:49,669 --> 00:02:51,354
When agents finally went inside,

86
00:02:51,620 --> 00:02:53,150
they found what they expected:

87
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:55,280
an indoor growing operation with more

88
00:02:55,280 --> 00:02:57,148
than a hundred marijuana plants.

89
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:00,050
Kyllo was charged with manufacturing
marijuana.

90
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:03,200
He asked the court to throw the evidence
out, arguing that the thermal

91
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:05,480
scan itself was an illegal search

92
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:06,380
of his home.

93
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:08,547
When that failed, he pleaded guilty —

94
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:10,060
but kept the right to appeal

95
00:03:10,060 --> 00:03:11,957
the one question that mattered.

96
00:03:12,060 --> 00:03:14,260
And it was a genuinely hard question.

97
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,105
Think about what the agent actually did.

98
00:03:17,300 --> 00:03:18,740
He stood on a public street,

99
00:03:18,740 --> 00:03:20,580
where anyone is allowed to be.

100
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:23,460
He aimed a device at the outside of a house.

101
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:26,780
He measured heat that was already
leaking out into the open air.

102
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:28,808
He never touched the home,

103
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:31,180
never entered it, never even set foot

104
00:03:31,180 --> 00:03:32,412
on the property.

105
00:03:32,560 --> 00:03:33,580
By the old rules,

106
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:35,389
that looked like no search at all.

107
00:03:35,740 --> 00:03:37,003
For most of its history,

108
00:03:37,180 --> 00:03:39,706
the Fourth Amendment was
about physical trespass

109
00:03:40,501 --> 00:03:40,801
—

110
00:03:40,802 --> 00:03:43,281
an officer breaking in, going
through your drawers,

111
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:44,948
carrying your things away.

112
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:46,760
Elliott did none of that.

113
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:50,420
He simply gathered heat that the house
was giving off on its own.

114
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,316
But push a little harder,

115
00:03:52,520 --> 00:03:54,467
and it starts to feel very different.

116
00:03:55,020 --> 00:03:56,520
The entire point of the scan

117
00:03:56,520 --> 00:03:58,260
was to learn what was happening inside

118
00:03:58,260 --> 00:03:59,527
a private home —

119
00:03:59,640 --> 00:04:01,480
something the agent could not see

120
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:03,269
with his own eyes from the street.

121
00:04:03,460 --> 00:04:05,280
He used a machine to pull private

122
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:06,520
facts out of the one place

123
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:08,467
the Constitution guards most closely.

124
00:04:09,040 --> 00:04:10,987
If the government can do that freely,

125
00:04:11,020 --> 00:04:15,247
then the wall around your home is only as
private as the next new sensor allows.

126
00:04:15,620 --> 00:04:16,520
So which is it?

127
00:04:16,660 --> 00:04:19,202
A harmless reading of heat in the open air —

128
00:04:19,203 --> 00:04:21,100
or a search of your home by another name?

129
00:04:21,580 --> 00:04:23,160
On June 11, 2001,

130
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:25,150
the Supreme Court gave its answer,

131
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:26,919
and it was close —

132
00:04:26,920 --> 00:04:28,620
five justices to four.

133
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:31,000
The majority opinion came
from Justice Antonin

134
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,220
Scalia, and the lineup behind it did

135
00:04:33,220 --> 00:04:35,799
not follow the usual conservative-liberal
script;

136
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:38,220
justices who rarely agreed stood

137
00:04:38,220 --> 00:04:39,120
on the same side.

138
00:04:39,620 --> 00:04:41,252
This was not ordinary politics.

139
00:04:41,550 --> 00:04:43,760
It was a fight about the
meaning of the home.

140
00:04:44,520 --> 00:04:46,362
Scalia began with first principles.

141
00:04:46,440 --> 00:04:48,280
"At the very core" of the Fourth

142
00:04:48,280 --> 00:04:50,840
Amendment, he wrote, "stands the right

143
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:52,140
of a man to retreat

144
00:04:52,140 --> 00:04:53,960
into his own home and there

145
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:56,900
be free from unreasonable
governmental intrusion."

146
00:04:57,600 --> 00:04:59,460
The home is not just another place

147
00:04:59,460 --> 00:05:00,723
the police might search.

148
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:01,920
It is the place.

149
00:05:02,480 --> 00:05:03,690
And the Fourth Amendment,

150
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:05,900
the Court said, draws a firm line

151
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:07,381
at its entrance —

152
00:05:07,382 --> 00:05:09,260
a line that must be "not only

153
00:05:09,260 --> 00:05:10,720
firm but also bright."

154
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:13,740
Then came the rule that
made the case famous.

155
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:16,060
Where the government uses a device

156
00:05:16,060 --> 00:05:17,760
that is not in general public use

157
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:19,860
to explore details of a home

158
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:21,680
that it could not have learned without

159
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:24,480
physically going inside, that is a search

160
00:05:25,478 --> 00:05:25,778
.

161
00:05:25,779 --> 00:05:29,360
And a search of a home, without a warrant,
is presumptively unreasonable

162
00:05:29,747 --> 00:05:30,350
.

163
00:05:30,380 --> 00:05:32,222
Notice how carefully that is built.

164
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:33,880
It does not turn on whether

165
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:35,200
the officer trespassed.

166
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:37,860
It turns on the technology reaching past

167
00:05:37,860 --> 00:05:39,760
the wall to reveal the inside.

168
00:05:40,740 --> 00:05:43,020
Scalia's worry looked forward, not back.

169
00:05:43,500 --> 00:05:45,480
If the rule depended only on how

170
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:47,620
good today's device happened to be,

171
00:05:47,760 --> 00:05:49,460
then privacy in the home would shrink

172
00:05:49,460 --> 00:05:51,680
a little every time a better
machine came along.

173
00:05:51,740 --> 00:05:52,980
So the Court fixed the line

174
00:05:52,980 --> 00:05:53,980
at the wall itself:

175
00:05:54,560 --> 00:05:56,120
what the inside of your home holds

176
00:05:56,120 --> 00:05:58,680
is presumptively yours, and pulling it out

177
00:05:58,680 --> 00:06:00,417
with a special device is a search

178
00:06:00,575 --> 00:06:01,090
—

179
00:06:01,120 --> 00:06:02,620
whether that device reads heat,

180
00:06:02,620 --> 00:06:04,180
or something no one has invented

181
00:06:04,180 --> 00:06:05,080
yet.

182
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:08,300
Four justices disagreed, and their argument

183
00:06:08,300 --> 00:06:09,280
was not foolish.

184
00:06:10,040 --> 00:06:12,500
Justice John Paul Stevens wrote the dissent

185
00:06:12,501 --> 00:06:13,250
.

186
00:06:13,280 --> 00:06:14,440
The agent, he said,

187
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:17,960
never went through the wall. He only
measured heat that had already

188
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:19,600
radiated off the outside surface

189
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:21,140
of the house and drifted

190
00:06:21,140 --> 00:06:22,193
into the public air.

191
00:06:22,920 --> 00:06:26,020
That, Stevens argued, was "off-the-wall"
observation,

192
00:06:26,540 --> 00:06:28,487
not "through-the-wall" surveillance —

193
00:06:28,780 --> 00:06:30,760
closer to smelling an odor that floats

194
00:06:30,760 --> 00:06:32,880
out of a kitchen than to peering

195
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:33,780
inside a room.

196
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:36,010
Heat that leaves your house,

197
00:06:36,040 --> 00:06:37,800
in his view, is already out

198
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:39,700
in the world, and collecting it should

199
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:41,689
not count as searching your home.

200
00:06:41,940 --> 00:06:43,256
It is a real distinction.

201
00:06:43,700 --> 00:06:46,700
But the majority would not rest
the privacy of the home on it.

202
00:06:47,240 --> 00:06:48,661
Whatever you call the heat,

203
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:50,720
the purpose and the result were the same:

204
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:54,395
the government learned what was going
on inside a house without a warrant.

205
00:06:54,680 --> 00:06:56,820
And that, five justices held,

206
00:06:56,820 --> 00:06:58,840
is exactly what the Fourth Amendment

207
00:06:58,840 --> 00:07:00,366
is there to stop.

208
00:07:00,440 --> 00:07:02,220
It is worth being precise about what

209
00:07:02,220 --> 00:07:03,760
the Court did, and what it did

210
00:07:03,760 --> 00:07:04,650
not do.

211
00:07:04,680 --> 00:07:06,480
It did not ban thermal imaging.

212
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:09,020
It did not erase the evidence against

213
00:07:09,020 --> 00:07:09,920
Kyllo for good.

214
00:07:10,360 --> 00:07:11,720
It sent the case back down

215
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:13,194
with a narrower instruction:

216
00:07:13,660 --> 00:07:15,380
this kind of scan is a search,

217
00:07:15,440 --> 00:07:17,127
so it needs a warrant —

218
00:07:17,128 --> 00:07:18,440
and the lower court now had

219
00:07:18,440 --> 00:07:20,400
to decide whether the warrant still stood

220
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:22,120
up once you set the thermal evidence

221
00:07:22,120 --> 00:07:23,690
aside.

222
00:07:23,691 --> 00:07:27,120
The lesson was not "you can never
do this." It was "get a warrant

223
00:07:27,120 --> 00:07:28,010
first."

224
00:07:28,040 --> 00:07:29,882
That difference is the whole point.

225
00:07:30,540 --> 00:07:32,680
The Constitution rarely tells the police

226
00:07:32,680 --> 00:07:34,312
they can never learn something.

227
00:07:34,760 --> 00:07:35,865
Usually it just says:

228
00:07:36,240 --> 00:07:37,140
go to a judge,

229
00:07:37,240 --> 00:07:39,480
show your reasons, and get permission before

230
00:07:39,480 --> 00:07:41,112
you reach into a private space.

231
00:07:42,020 --> 00:07:44,886
Kyllo pulled the home firmly
back inside that rule.

232
00:07:45,160 --> 00:07:48,620
And the reason the case still matters
lies in the phrase Scalia chose:

233
00:07:49,380 --> 00:07:51,840
technology "not in general public use."

234
00:07:52,520 --> 00:07:54,560
In 1992, the fight was over

235
00:07:54,560 --> 00:07:55,620
a heat camera the size

236
00:07:55,620 --> 00:07:56,520
of a lunchbox.

237
00:07:56,790 --> 00:07:58,740
But the logic reaches much further.

238
00:07:59,680 --> 00:08:01,620
Every year, the tools for sensing what

239
00:08:01,620 --> 00:08:03,420
happens inside a home get cheaper,

240
00:08:03,980 --> 00:08:05,690
smaller, and sharper —

241
00:08:06,080 --> 00:08:07,940
drones overhead, better cameras,

242
00:08:08,500 --> 00:08:10,605
devices that read signals through walls.

243
00:08:11,640 --> 00:08:13,440
Kyllo is the case courts keep returning

244
00:08:13,440 --> 00:08:15,100
to when they ask how far

245
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:17,480
the government can push those tools before

246
00:08:17,480 --> 00:08:18,846
it needs a warrant.

247
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:22,780
The strength of the ruling, and its
weakness, is that same phrase.

248
00:08:23,400 --> 00:08:25,080
"Not in general public use"

249
00:08:25,080 --> 00:08:26,000
is a moving line.

250
00:08:26,800 --> 00:08:28,960
A device that is exotic today can

251
00:08:28,960 --> 00:08:31,420
sit in everyone's pocket tomorrow —

252
00:08:31,480 --> 00:08:33,743
and the more ordinary a technology becomes,

253
00:08:34,280 --> 00:08:36,160
the weaker your claim that using

254
00:08:36,160 --> 00:08:37,890
it invades your privacy.

255
00:08:38,380 --> 00:08:40,300
The Court protected the home against

256
00:08:40,300 --> 00:08:42,780
the future, but it tied that protection

257
00:08:42,780 --> 00:08:44,885
to a standard the future keeps rewriting

258
00:08:45,138 --> 00:08:45,590
.

259
00:08:45,620 --> 00:08:46,820
So go back to that agent

260
00:08:46,820 --> 00:08:48,980
in the car, on the freezing street,

261
00:08:48,980 --> 00:08:50,875
a little after three in the morning.

262
00:08:51,280 --> 00:08:52,180
He never knocked.

263
00:08:52,500 --> 00:08:53,868
He never crossed the lawn.

264
00:08:54,340 --> 00:08:57,940
He simply read the heat coming off a stranger's
walls and decided he knew

265
00:08:57,940 --> 00:09:00,402
what was inside.

266
00:09:00,403 --> 00:09:03,575
The Supreme Court looked at that
quiet, no-contact search —

267
00:09:03,620 --> 00:09:04,936
a search with no break-in

268
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:07,065
and no trespass at all —

269
00:09:07,420 --> 00:09:09,100
and said the home draws a bright

270
00:09:09,100 --> 00:09:11,460
line, and the government had stepped over

271
00:09:11,460 --> 00:09:12,290
it.

272
00:09:12,320 --> 00:09:14,215
But notice what saved the home here:

273
00:09:14,740 --> 00:09:15,350
walls.

274
00:09:15,380 --> 00:09:17,360
Something solid for the heat to leak

275
00:09:17,360 --> 00:09:19,900
through, an edge the Court could point

276
00:09:19,900 --> 00:09:21,220
to and call the entrance.

277
00:09:21,780 --> 00:09:24,200
The Fourth Amendment grew
up protecting exactly

278
00:09:24,200 --> 00:09:26,175
that —

279
00:09:26,176 --> 00:09:28,420
houses, walls, doors, the ground you stand

280
00:09:28,420 --> 00:09:29,320
on.

281
00:09:29,480 --> 00:09:33,548
So what happens when the thing the government
wants to reach has no walls around it at all?

282
00:09:34,120 --> 00:09:35,940
When your private words travel as sound

283
00:09:35,940 --> 00:09:38,760
through the open air, or
as a signal down a wire

284
00:09:41,079 --> 00:09:41,379
—

285
00:09:41,380 --> 00:09:44,090
into a glass phone booth on a public
sidewalk, where anyone can see you,

286
00:09:43,740 --> 00:09:44,740
but no one is supposed

287
00:09:44,740 --> 00:09:45,640
to be listening?

288
00:09:46,300 --> 00:09:47,740
Long before the heat camera,

289
00:09:47,740 --> 00:09:49,720
the Court faced that exact question,

290
00:09:49,720 --> 00:09:51,620
about a man, a booth,

291
00:09:51,880 --> 00:09:53,143
and a hidden microphone.

292
00:09:53,580 --> 00:09:57,720
The answer moved the Fourth Amendment from
protecting places to protecting people.

293
00:09:58,320 --> 00:09:59,300
That is next time.
